course: dictionary
lesson: 6366
title: Much and how much
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Much means a lot of something that you cannot count one by one, like much water or much money. «There is not much milk left in the jug.» You cannot count milk one drop at a time, so much tells you how big the amount is.
explain: «How much» asks for an amount or a price. «How much is this book?» means what does the book cost? Ask yourself: is this something I could count, like books or apples? If yes, use many. If no, use much.
explain: Much can also make a comparison stronger. «The new bike is much faster than my old one.» The word much tells you the difference is big, not small.
fact: Use much with things you cannot count, like water, money and time.
fact: «How much» asks for an amount or a price.
fact: «Not much» means only a little, and «too much» means more than you need or want.
fact: «Much» can make a comparison stronger, as in «much bigger» or «much faster».
fact: «Much of» means a large part of something: «Much of Earth's fresh water is frozen in ice sheets» means a large part of it is frozen.
exercise: «There is not much milk left in the jug.» What does not much mean here? | A great deal | *Only a little | Full to the top | Cold
exercise: «How much is this book?» What does how much ask for? | The name of the writer | The date | The colour | *The price
exercise: Which word goes in the gap? «The new bike is ___ faster than my old one.» | *much | in | very | the
test: «If it gets too much, tell a parent or a teacher.» What does too much mean? | *More than you can cope with | Exactly the right amount | A kind of food | Not enough to notice
test: Which word goes in the gap? «Mia poured ___ water into the bucket.» | three | many | *much | few
exam: In «Much of Earth's fresh water is frozen in ice sheets», what does much mean? | *A large part | Very cold | Exactly one | Nothing at all
